{%- set faqs = [
  ["What is deepfake security?", "Deepfake security is the practice of protecting people, processes and systems from AI-generated fake faces, voices and videos used to deceive. It covers testing whether identity checks, approval procedures and support teams can be fooled, and strengthening the controls that decide who is trusted."],
  ["How do attackers use deepfakes in fraud?", "Common uses include a cloned voice or video of an executive instructing someone to make a payment, a synthetic face that passes remote identity verification to open an account, and a fake caller who persuades a help desk to reset credentials. In each case the attacker borrows trust that a person's face or voice would normally carry."],
  ["What is synthetic identity fraud?", "Synthetic identity fraud uses a made-up identity, often a mix of real and invented details, to open accounts or obtain credit. AI tools now generate convincing photos, documents and even short videos to support those identities, which makes onboarding checks a frequent target."],
  ["Can liveness detection be bypassed?", "Some implementations can. Attackers try presentation attacks, such as a photo, a screen replay or a mask, and injection attacks, in which a virtual camera or modified app feeds fake video straight into the check. Testing shows which of these your solution resists and where it needs strengthening."],
  ["What is KYC and identity verification testing?", "It is a security assessment of the flows that confirm a person's identity, such as document upload, selfie matching, liveness checks and database lookups. We test whether forged documents, synthetic faces and automated attacks can pass, and whether the process fails safely when they are suspected."],
  ["Do you create deepfakes as part of testing?", "Yes, where it is authorized. Simulations use consenting participants or fully synthetic personas, never a real person's likeness without their written permission, and follow rules of engagement agreed in advance. Executives whose voice or image would be used must approve it."],
  ["Can you detect deepfakes for us?", "Our focus is on testing and strengthening the controls around identity and approvals, which matter whether or not a fake is detected. We can review the detection tools you use or are evaluating, and recommend process controls that still protect you when detection fails."],
  ["Can voice authentication be fooled by voice cloning?", "Voice cloning can reproduce a speaker closely enough to challenge systems and staff that rely on how someone sounds. We test whether voice biometrics, call-center checks and call-back procedures hold up, and recommend controls that do not depend on voice alone."],
  ["Do you test help desk and employee processes?", "Yes. Attackers often target support staff who can reset passwords or multi-factor authentication. We test whether callers can be verified reliably, whether urgency and authority cause staff to skip steps, and whether sensitive resets require strong proof."],
  ["How can businesses defend against deepfake payment fraud?", "The most effective defenses are procedural: independent call-back to a known number, dual approval for large or unusual payments, delays for changes to payment details and a culture where staff are encouraged to verify. Testing checks whether those safeguards work under pressure."],
  ["How long does a digital identity and deepfake assessment take?", "Duration depends on the number of identity journeys, channels and teams in scope. A focused assessment of one onboarding flow can be short, while a program covering customers, employees and payments takes longer. We confirm the timeline after scoping."],
  ["How much does it cost?", "Cost depends on the number of journeys and channels, the depth of simulation and the reporting scope. Share a short description of your identity flows with CipherTrivia and we will recommend a scope and quote."],
  ["What does the report include?", "The report includes an executive summary, a map of the identity journeys tested, each finding with its severity, the steps to reproduce it, supporting evidence and a recommended fix. Findings can feed policy changes, product fixes and staff training."],
  ["Do you offer retesting after controls are improved?", "Yes. Retesting can be included to confirm that fixes work against the same attacks and that new controls have not created new gaps. The retest results are added to the report."]
] %}

